WEBVTT

s1.1
00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.378
The merger takes a pull request from ready to merged.

s1.2
00:00:03.378 --> 00:00:08.477
One merger serves every repository that has a merger policy on its default branch.

s1.3
00:00:08.477 --> 00:00:12.123
Each repository keeps its own train, lease and review workers.

s1.4
00:00:12.123 --> 00:00:17.079
No outside review service takes part. A person approves only if the policy asks.

s1.5
00:00:17.079 --> 00:00:20.619
First, agents review the pull request and write one report.

s1.6
00:00:20.619 --> 00:00:24.413
Then a deterministic merge train rebases, tests and merges it.

s1.7
00:00:24.413 --> 00:00:27.835
Agents help in the train only when a rebase conflicts.

s2.1
00:00:28.185 --> 00:00:33.826
GitHub webhooks wake the merger: pull requests, check runs, workflow runs and pushes to main.

s2.2
00:00:33.826 --> 00:00:36.722
Each webhook wakes only the loops of its repository.

s2.3
00:00:36.722 --> 00:00:40.475
If a webhook is lost, a safety poll runs every 30 seconds.

s2.4
00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:45.812
Each repository gets two review workers by default, beside its train loop and main watch.

s2.5
00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:49.244
Across all repositories, at most eight reviews run at once.

s2.6
00:00:49.244 --> 00:00:52.420
A heartbeat tells the API that the merger is alive.

s2.7
00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:56.722
A worker claims a pull request for two minutes, and renews the claim while it works.

s2.8
00:00:56.722 --> 00:01:01.183
If a pod dies, its claims expire, and another worker takes the pull request.

s2.9
00:01:01.183 --> 00:01:05.792
The queue takes bypassed pull requests first, then re-reviews, then first reviews.

s2.10
00:01:05.792 --> 00:01:09.057
In each group, the oldest ready pull request goes first.

s2.11
00:01:09.057 --> 00:01:15.446
A pull request is eligible when it is open, ready, based on the default branch, and its head has a check run.

s2.12
00:01:15.446 --> 00:01:20.790
By default, it also needs the symphony or symphony-learnings label, or review-bypass.

s3.1
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:24.236
Before a review, the merger probes the model of each role.

s3.2
00:01:24.236 --> 00:01:27.720
The agent must read a secret value from a file and return it.

s3.3
00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.330
If a probe fails, the worker waits from one minute up to ten minutes.

s3.4
00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:33.408
Next come deterministic prechecks.

s3.5
00:01:33.408 --> 00:01:37.910
They catch fixup commits, large files, local paths, broken links and secrets.

s3.6
00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:41.316
The repository can add its own checks, such as a lint command.

s3.7
00:01:41.316 --> 00:01:44.213
Then reviewer agents read the diff in parallel lanes.

s3.8
00:01:44.213 --> 00:01:47.861
The default lanes are correctness, security and spec, and design.

s3.9
00:01:47.861 --> 00:01:51.684
A reviewer reports each finding through a tool call with a fixed schema.

s3.10
00:01:51.684 --> 00:01:55.259
An oracle on a different model checks the findings, four per call.

s3.11
00:01:55.259 --> 00:01:59.499
A finding blocks only when the reviewer and the oracle both grade it blocking.

s3.12
00:01:59.499 --> 00:02:03.039
These agents can only read and search. They cannot edit.

s4.1
00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:06.447
During a review, the pull request has the in-audit label.

s4.2
00:02:06.447 --> 00:02:09.370
Each review ends in exactly one report comment.

s4.3
00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:12.820
Its title is Reviewed, Not merged, or Waiting for CI.

s4.4
00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:18.373
Then come the merger, the head commit, the outcome, the CI line, and one section for each finding.

s4.5
00:02:18.373 --> 00:02:22.526
If CI still runs, the merger posts the report now and parks the review.

s4.6
00:02:22.526 --> 00:02:25.147
When CI ends, it edits that same comment.

s4.7
00:02:25.147 --> 00:02:28.889
Green CI hands the pull request to the train, with the reviewed label.

s4.8
00:02:28.889 --> 00:02:34.241
If the policy requires approval, a green pull request first waits for an approval on GitHub.

s4.9
00:02:34.241 --> 00:02:39.719
It gets the wait-approval label and shows as waiting for approval. Its reviewers get one notice.

s4.10
00:02:39.719 --> 00:02:43.802
A confirmed block, a rule break or red CI sends it back to the author.

s4.11
00:02:43.802 --> 00:02:49.054
The first time, it goes to draft with the review-requested label, and the ticket goes to Rework.

s4.12
00:02:49.054 --> 00:02:53.576
Its findings also land on their diff lines, in one review that only comments.

s4.13
00:02:53.576 --> 00:02:57.158
This happens once. A later block leaves the pull request ready.

s4.14
00:02:57.158 --> 00:03:01.625
With polish rounds, an agent's pull request with fixable nits goes back too.

s4.15
00:03:01.625 --> 00:03:05.907
A failed run writes no comment. Only the team channel hears about it.

s5.1
00:03:06.257 --> 00:03:09.502
The merger keeps watching after it sends a pull request back.

s5.2
00:03:09.502 --> 00:03:12.649
The author pushes a fix and marks the pull request ready.

s5.3
00:03:12.649 --> 00:03:15.801
Now it is a re-review, so it goes ahead of first reviews.

s5.4
00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:19.040
One reviewer reads only what changed since the reviewed head.

s5.5
00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.559
If the author rebased, the merger first replays the reviewed commits onto the new main.

s5.6
00:03:23.559 --> 00:03:26.288
So the delta does not contain the changes of main.

s5.7
00:03:26.288 --> 00:03:33.052
After the one rejection, a finding blocks only if it reopens an earlier one, or is a bug near the new changes.

s5.8
00:03:33.052 --> 00:03:36.275
A block that is confirmed again goes to an AI arbiter.

s5.9
00:03:36.275 --> 00:03:41.095
If the arbiter upholds it, the needs-maintainer label goes on, and a person decides.

s5.10
00:03:41.095 --> 00:03:45.486
If the arbiter dismisses every finding, the pull request goes on to the train.

s5.11
00:03:45.486 --> 00:03:50.641
If the author pushes during a review, the review stops. The next review covers only the push.

s5.12
00:03:50.641 --> 00:03:55.467
If the ticket is canceled, the merger comments once and closes its pull requests.

s6.1
00:03:55.817 --> 00:03:58.554
The train runs one step at a time, under a lease.

s6.2
00:03:58.554 --> 00:04:03.272
It takes first the pull request that overlaps least with the recent changes on main.

s6.3
00:04:03.272 --> 00:04:06.996
It rebases the branch onto main and pushes it with force-with-lease.

s6.4
00:04:06.996 --> 00:04:12.686
It waits for CI on that exact head. A webhook wakes it, and it also checks every 30 seconds.

s6.5
00:04:12.686 --> 00:04:17.343
It merges only that tested head, posts a Merged comment, and deletes the branch.

s6.6
00:04:17.343 --> 00:04:20.919
When several pull requests wait, it chains up to four in a batch.

s6.7
00:04:20.919 --> 00:04:24.124
CI runs only on the top, and the members merge in order.

s6.8
00:04:24.124 --> 00:04:30.251
A red batch reruns its failed jobs once. Then it splits in halves until the bad pull request is alone.

s6.9
00:04:30.251 --> 00:04:35.894
If a rebase conflicts, a developer agent resolves it, and a reviewer agent checks the result.

s6.10
00:04:35.894 --> 00:04:40.413
Then the pull request gets a Rebased onto main comment and goes back to its place.

s6.11
00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:44.829
Before each effect, the train saves its intent, so a restart repeats nothing.

s6.12
00:04:44.829 --> 00:04:50.096
At hand-off, the ticket moves to Merging. After the merge, the tracker moves it to Done.

s7.1
00:04:50.446 --> 00:04:53.393
The main watch reads each finished CI run on main.

s7.2
00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:57.598
When main turns red, it opens one ticket with the logs of the failed jobs.

s7.3
00:04:57.598 --> 00:05:02.265
The ticket has the routing labels, so the daemon dispatches an agent to fix main.

s7.4
00:05:02.265 --> 00:05:06.029
When main is green again, it comments on the ticket and moves it to Done.

s7.5
00:05:06.029 --> 00:05:08.704
Both events also go to Slack or Discord.

s8.1
00:05:09.054 --> 00:05:12.264
Each repository sets its rules in a policy file.

s8.2
00:05:12.264 --> 00:05:17.720
The merger reads that file from the default branch, so a pull request cannot weaken its own review.

s8.3
00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:23.586
The file sets the lanes, the checks, the merge method, the batch size, an approval gate and polish rounds.

s8.4
00:05:23.586 --> 00:05:30.178
With a preview build in CI, the merger posts a button in Slack and keeps one Preview comment, with a link per build.

s8.5
00:05:30.178 --> 00:05:34.760
Operators can bypass a review, requeue a pull request, or release a stuck claim.

s8.6
00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.373
They use the CLI, the API, or the Merger view in Tern.

s8.7
00:05:38.373 --> 00:05:42.704
Each merger agent is a live session that you can follow and interrupt.

s9.1
00:05:43.054 --> 00:05:48.697
The merger was certified on a real GitHub repository, with real CI and real agents.

s9.2
00:05:48.697 --> 00:05:51.414
A clean pull request merged at the reviewed head.

s9.3
00:05:51.414 --> 00:05:55.041
A real bug went back to draft once, with a confirmed finding.

s9.4
00:05:55.041 --> 00:05:58.801
Agents resolved a rebase conflict, and the pull request merged.

s9.5
00:05:58.801 --> 00:06:03.428
Batches of three merged with CI on the top only, also on a protected main.

s9.6
00:06:03.428 --> 00:06:06.672
With no findings, a review took 10 to 40 seconds.

s9.7
00:06:06.672 --> 00:06:10.760
In the mocked runs, the merger reviewed and merged on both trackers.
